{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3164","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3164","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3164\",\"parent_revision\":\"80f9395e8ae93a077342da6d41176399b0565f3e46d0b0c294506e08309fae87\",\"root_revision\":\"80f9395e8ae93a077342da6d41176399b0565f3e46d0b0c294506e08309fae87\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-22\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`backlog_core/tests/unit/progressive_markdown/test_indexer_decomposition.py::TestPLRLintGate::test_indexer_build_passes_plr_complexity_gates` fails with `FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'ruff'`.\\n\\nThe test shells out to a `ruff` binary that is not present on PATH in the environment the standalone plugin runner creates. It fails identically on a branch and on a clean `main`, and reproduced independently in three separate agent sessions, each of which recorded it as a pre-existing failure unrelated to their change.\\n\\nObserved 2026-08-22 and 2026-08-23. The rest of the suite passes: roughly 4442 passed, 42 skipped, 5 xfailed alongside it.\\n\\nImpact: every agent running the plugin suite sees a red result it must first diagnose and then decide to ignore. A permanently-failing test trains readers to discount failures, and hides a genuine regression in the same file behind a known-red line. It also means the lint gate this test exists to enforce is not actually being enforced in that environment \u2014 the test cannot be measuring what it claims when the binary it measures with is absent.\\n\\nNot established: whether the fix is to declare the dependency in the runner's environment, to invoke the linter through the project's managed environment rather than a bare binary lookup, or to skip the test when the binary is unavailable. The third would silence the signal rather than restore the gate.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 reproduced independently in three agent sessions, 2026-08-22 and 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-22\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Correction The Stated Cause Does Not Reproduce And The Reports Disagree\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRun directly against the current tree on 2026-08-23:\\n\\n```\\nuv run pytest plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/progressive_markdown/test_indexer_decomposition.py::TestPLRLintGate -x -q\\n1 passed in 109.52s\\n```\\n\\nIt passes. The `FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'ruff'` stated above was reported by one agent session; a second session reporting the same test as failing described it instead as a PLR complexity gate failing on the indexer module. Two different causes for one symptom, and neither reproduces on a direct run.\\n\\nWhat is actually established:\\n\\n- The test fails intermittently across agent sessions running the plugin's standalone runner, and has been reported that way independently more than once.\\n- It passes when invoked directly through the repository's own pytest configuration.\\n- The two reported causes are mutually inconsistent, so at least one is a misattribution.\\n\\nThe difference between the two invocation paths is the likely place to look: the standalone runner (`plugins/development-harness/tests/run_pytest.py`) changes directory to the plugin root and constructs its own argument set, so environment and PATH differ from a repository-root run. Whether that accounts for either reported failure is not established.\\n\\nThis issue should not be worked from the cause stated above. Reproduce it first through the standalone runner, capture the actual failure output, and only then diagnose. A test that fails in one harness and passes in another, with two conflicting explanations on record, is a reproduction problem before it is a fix.\\n\\nThe impact stated above still holds regardless of cause: agents running the plugin suite see a red result they must diagnose and then choose to ignore, which trains readers to discount failures.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"8dddc5b1482a90124aed8d97f3bb17d55fc4af4e95e80ed02cb9b90844bd3d71\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQOH8Dw\"}"}